  "domain": "blockchain",
  "definition": "A Hash Time-Locked Contract (HTLC) is a type of smart contract that conditionally releases funds to a recipient only if they present a valid cryptographic preimage satisfying a specified hash condition within a defined time window; if the condition remains unmet before the timeout expires, the funds automatically revert to the sender. HTLCs combine two complementary mechanisms\u2014a hashlock, which binds settlement to knowledge of a secret, and a timelock, which enforces a bounded settlement window\u2014to achieve trustless atomicity across one or more blockchain ledgers. They are foundational to payment channel networks such as the Lightning Network and to cross-chain atomic swap protocols, enabling conditional payment routing without custodial intermediaries or mutual trust. As a composable on-chain primitive, HTLCs underpin a wide range of decentralised finance and interoperability constructs.",
